# Claude Code

@AGENTS.md

<!-- ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     THIS FILE IS A POINTER. That is its whole job.

     AGENTS.md is the canonical instruction file because every other tool
     (Codex, Gemini, Cursor, opencode) reads it directly and only Claude Code
     reads CLAUDE.md. One file means every tool gets identical rules.

     Never put a rule here. A rule in CLAUDE.md is a rule that Claude follows
     and no other CLI can see — which is exactly how two agents on the same
     repo start disagreeing, with nothing in the diff to explain why.

     Some repos add a few lines of prose above the import, for the case where
     an agent was told "read ./CLAUDE.md" and does a plain file read (the
     @import does not expand for that). That is fine — it is signage, not
     rules. Keep it to a sentence or two:

         **All project instructions live in `AGENTS.md` in this same
         directory. Read that file now — this one is only a pointer.**
     ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
